== Serving Together in Family Ministry

=== §. 40. #emph[Of husbands’ and wives’ joint care in governing the family.]

So far I have set out such common duties as concern the husband and wife mutually, and are to be performed by each to the other. There are other common duties which they are both jointly bound to perform to other persons: either to #emph[members] of the family, or to #emph[strangers] coming to the family.

Concerning the #emph[members] of the family: though there are some distinctive duties belonging to the master and some to the mistress, some to the father and some to the mother, of which we will speak in their due place, yet in general the government of the family and of its separate members belongs to both husband and wife (if, at least, they have a family). And it is a joint, common duty of theirs to be helpful to one another in it.

#emph[Objection.] Since it is not necessarily required that a husband and wife should have a family to govern, for two may be married and have neither children nor servants (as many are) and yet be true husband and wife, why is this care of a family ranked among the duties of husband and wife?

#emph[Answer.] 1. Because ordinarily, when two are married, they gather a family and are its governors. So though it fails in some particular cases, yet for the most part it holds.

2\. Because the joint government of which I speak in this place is by virtue of the marriage bond. For if a man and a woman should live together in a house and, by mutual consent, have a joint authority and government, this would be very offensive to all who should know of it or hear of it. Nor would they be bound by any ordinance of God to be as helpful to one another as husband and wife, nor would the members of a family be so bound to subject themselves to both.

3\. The duty of which I speak, though it is about the government of a family, yet concerns a husband as he is a husband, and a wife as she is a wife: namely, that by virtue of their marriage bond and close union they should be helpful to one another in well ordering the things of the family.

Whether the man ought to see to the good government of his house is a question beyond all question. He is the highest in the family; he has authority over all, and the charge of all is committed to him. He is like a king in his own house: as a king is to see that the land where he is king is well governed, so is he who is the chief ruler in a house. The duty which the apostle applies in particular to bishops and deacons belongs in general to all husbands: #footnote[1 Tim 3:4.] that each “must manage his own household well”; and again, #footnote[#emph[v.] 12.] that each be “a good manager of his children and of his own household.” The care of many husbands in this respect is commended in Scripture: that of Abram,#footnote[Gen 18:19.] of Jacob,#footnote[Gen 35:2, #emph[etc.]] of Joshua,#footnote[Josh 24:15.] of David,#footnote[Ps 101:2. #emph[etc.]] of the #footnote[John 4:53.] ruler at Galilee, and of many others. That the wife also ought to be a help to him in this is very evident. For the apostle expressly lays it to the charge of wives #footnote[1 Tim 5:14.] that they “govern their households.” Would the wise man have so highly commended a wife #footnote[Prov 31:11, #emph[etc.]] for governing her husband’s house well if it had not belonged to her? It is very likely that wise Abigail had a great hand in governing Nabal’s house: because the servants complained to her of Nabal’s churlishness; because she had the servants at her command, ready to do what she wanted of them; and also because she could so readily prepare such a store of provisions for David and his men as she did. That is why the wife is called #emph[mistress of the house,]#footnote[Mater-familias.] just as the husband is called #emph[master of the house.]#footnote[Pater-familias.]

#emph[Objection.] A woman is not to teach or to usurp authority over a man.#footnote[1 Tim 2:12.]

#emph[Answer.] 1. That branch about #emph[teaching] refers to public assemblies and churches, in which she must not #emph[teach,] but not to private families, in which she may and ought to #emph[teach.] For Bathsheba#footnote[Prov 31:4.] taught Solomon. When Apollos#footnote[Acts 18:26.] was brought to the house of Aquila, Priscilla, the wife of Aquila, helped to explain to Apollos the way of God more accurately.

2\. The other branch, concerning #emph[authority,] does not refer to the inferior members of the family, over whom the wife of a household governor has authority, but only to the husband: if she takes any #emph[authority] over him, she usurps it. Therefore neither this place of Scripture nor any other excludes the wife, considered jointly with the husband, from ruling and governing those in the family who are under them both.

2\. #emph[Objection.] This joint government of the wife greatly impairs the dignity and authority of the husband.

#emph[Answer.] Not at all. For she is subordinate to her husband, and must rule others in such a way that she is subject to her husband, and does not command anything against his command (provided that his command is not against the Lord and his word). We see that in every state the king or highest governor has other magistrates under him, who have command over the subjects; and yet the king’s supreme authority is not in the least impaired by this, but rather is the better established, and he is the more honored. So it is in a family. #footnote[1\. #emph[Reason.] Husbands and wives by their mutual assistance bring great help to one another. #emph[Plus vident oculi quàm oculus. Arist. Polit. li.] 3. #emph[c.] 7.]

Therefore let husbands and wives assist one another in this, for so they may be very helpful to one another and, by their mutual help in governing, bring much good to the family. The husband, aiding his wife by his help, adds much authority to her, and so ensures that she is not despised or lightly regarded. The wife by her help causes many things to be noticed, and so set right, which otherwise might never have been found out. For two eyes see more than one, especially when one of them is closer at hand and present, as the #footnote[2\. #emph[Reason.] Many things in a family are more fit for the one to deal with than for the other.] wife is in the house.

Besides, there are many things in governing a family well which are more fit for one to deal with than for the other. It is more fit for the husband to deal with the great and weighty affairs of the family (such as performing God’s worship, appointing and settling good orders, providing fitting house room and other necessities for the family, keeping children in awe when they grow #footnote[#emph[In familia dispertita sunt officia: suntque alia viri, alia vxoris propria. Prosunt ergo vterque alteri sua in commune conferentes: ob eamque causam suauitas in hac tali amicitia inest, vtilitasque maxima.] \[In a family the duties are divided, and some belong to the husband, others to the wife. So each helps the other by bringing his own into the common stock; and for this reason there is both pleasure and the greatest profit in such a friendship.\] #emph[Arist. Eth. li.] 8. #emph[ca.] 12.] older or become stubborn, ruling menservants, and the like). And it is more fit for the wife to deal with some lesser but very needful matters, such as nourishing and instructing children when they are young, adorning the house, ordering the provisions brought into the house, ruling maidservants, and the like. Yes, further, as the man especially is to perform the very acts of prayer, reading the word, catechizing and other such duties in the family, so the wife may be a great help: by putting her husband in mind both of the duty itself and of the time for performing it, and encouraging him to do it; by gathering the family together and exhorting them to be eager; by making herself an example to the rest through her diligent and reverent attention; by often urging and pressing on her children and servants such points of instruction as her husband has taught; yes, by praying, reading, teaching and performing such exercises herself, as far as she is able, when her husband is absent, or negligent and careless and will not do them himself, or perhaps is not able to do them; or, if she does not perform them herself, by getting someone else to perform them.

=== §. 41. #emph[Of the vices contrary to a joint care of governing the family.]

The mind and practice of many, both husbands and wives, #footnote[Vices contrary.] is contrary to this duty.

Many a husband, because the wife’s office is especially to #footnote[1\. When husbands hand over all government to their wives.] stay at home, will hand over all government to the wife. He leaves it to her not only to order the things in the house, but also to bring in all needful things, to order and govern the children both young and old, yes, even to provide for them too, and to take in, put out and use all sorts of servants as she pleases. Yes, if servants are stubborn and defiant toward her, he will take no notice of it, nor bear to be told of it, much less give her his assistance, but lets her be disgraced and despised. As for religious duties, he will have nothing at all to do with them. Oh base-minded men, unworthy to be husbands and heads of wives! Shall your#super[+] wives, who were made to be a help to you#super[+], have no help from you#super[+], no, not in those things which especially belong to your#super[+] charge? Shall the weaker vessels bear all the burden? Certainly, as the man carries away the greatest reputation and honor when a family is well governed (though it is by the joint care and wisdom of his wife), so he lies most open to the judgment of God if its government is neglected, and through that neglect children and servants grow ungodly. Take as an instance Eli#footnote[1 Sam 3:12, 13.] and David.#footnote[2 Sam 15.] For as in a commonwealth the greatest honor of good government, victorious battles, happy peace and prosperity, and the greatest dishonor and damage of the contrary, fall to the king, so it is with the man who is chief governor in a family. For it is presumed that all who do any good are instruments of the highest governor, and that if any evil or injury happens, it is through his negligence.

On the other side, because the husband is the chief, #footnote[2\. When wives refuse to add their aid.] many wives think that the government of the family does not belong to them at all. So they are careless of its good, and will not stir their least finger to set anything in order; but if anything is amiss, they lay all the blame on their husbands. Do not such wives pervert the main end for which God made them, which is #emph[to be a help?] Do they not behave most unworthily of the place in which God has set them, namely to be joint governors with their husbands and partakers of their dignities? As by their negligence they make themselves accessories to all the evil that happens in the family, so they will surely have their part in those judgments which are executed on its head.

Most contrary to the duty named above is the practice of #footnote[3\. When husbands or wives are hindrances to each other.] those who are hindrances to one another in governing the family. So it is when wives are not only negligent themselves in coming to religious exercises, but keep back children and servants too, and so are a great grief to their devout husbands; or when they use any of the children or servants as instruments of iniquity; or when they are themselves unquiet and troublesome in the house, like her of whom Solomon speaks in this proverb: “Better to live on a corner of the #footnote[Prov 21:9.] roof than to share a house with a quarrelsome wife.”

Husbands also are often a hindrance to the good government which their wives would help to further, when they scoff and scorn at the good counsel their wives give them for that purpose, or when they will not allow their wives to deal with anything at all, nor bear that they should find any fault, much less undertake to set right anything that is amiss. These and similar perverse dispositions are found in husbands and wives, and through them it comes about that those who were joined together to be a mutual help to each other prove heavy, yes, intolerable burdens.

=== §. 42. #emph[Of husbands’ and wives’ mutual help in hospitality.]

The next common duty of husband and wife concerns those who come to their house but are not particular members of it, whether they are kindred, relatives by marriage, acquaintances or strangers, especially if they are saints, to whom #emph[hospitality,]#footnote[Heb 13:2. Rom 12:13.] that is, a kind and courteous welcome, is due. In this, therefore, husbands and wives must be helpful to one another. For as it is required of #footnote[1 Tim 3:2.] husbands, so also of #footnote[1 Tim 5:10.] wives, to be “hospitable”: namely, while they are married, together with their husbands, and when they are widows, by themselves. Abraham#footnote[Gen 18:6. #emph[etc.]] and Sarah were a help to one another in this when the three angels came to their house in the shape of men; so were #footnote[2 Kgs 4:8.] the Shunammite woman and her husband when Elisha the prophet came to their house.

As for hospitality, that it is a commendable duty belonging #footnote[#emph[Reasons.] 1. Hospitality a commendable duty.] to those who are householders and able to entertain guests is evident from the precepts and examples specified above, and also from the blessing which God has brought on this account to the houses of those who were given to it. The apostle hints at this in these words: “by so doing some people have entertained angels without knowing it.”#footnote[Heb 13:2.] Now therefore, since husbands and wives are (as we have heard) joint governors, as in other things so in this they ought to lend a helping hand to each other, and that for these reasons.

1\. Because in entertaining guests there are various #footnote[2\. The hospitality will be the better for mutual help.] things to be done, some of which belong to the husband’s place and some to the wife’s. To see to the provision of things outside the house is more fit for the husband: Abraham#footnote[Gen 18:7.] did it. To order the smaller things inside the house is more fit for the wife: that was left to Sarah.#footnote[#emph[v.] 6.]

2\. Because it is fitting that guests should know they are welcome #footnote[3\. Guests will be more cheerful.] to both the husband and the wife, so that they may be all the more cheerful.

3\. Because mutual consent and cheerful help in this will #footnote[4\. Mutual love will be firmer.] be a special means both to show their mutual affection and to hold the hearts of man and wife firm and close together, and to make them like and love each other the better. This is especially so if the husband shows himself as ready and willing to entertain his wife’s friends and kindred as his own, and the wife likewise her husband’s (as they ought). For as they themselves are made one flesh, so each of them ought to regard the other’s friends as their own.

4\. Because in this way they show a mutual desire to bring #footnote[5\. A desire for one another’s good shown.] God’s blessing on each other and on their whole family.

=== §. 43. #emph[Of vices contrary to mutual help in hospitality.]

Contrary to this duty, for the most part, is covetousness in the husband and laziness in the wife. The man, because the charge of the family lies on him, distrustfully fears that he will not have enough for his own.

I do not deny that a provident care for our own, and especially for those of our family, is needful and commendable: he who does not provide for them “is worse than an unbeliever.”#footnote[1 Tim 5:8.] So a man may be too lavish in entertaining guests, if he goes beyond his means, impoverishes his estate and disables himself from providing for his own, as many do. Yet when a man has enough, yes, and abundance, and there is no just cause but only an undue fear, then to pinch too fretfully and distrustfully, and to begrudge entertaining anyone, is unbecoming in a Christian; yes, it is even to be worse than an unbeliever, not worthy to have a house or anything fit for entertaining guests, no, nor worthy of common society.

The woman, on the other side, resents the pains she must take and the trouble she must undergo in entertaining guests, and so is unwilling that anyone should ever come to their house.

Concerning a wife, I do not deny that a husband may be too burdensome to her in this way, by being too jovial, as they say, and bringing guests into the house too often, especially if they are guests of no good name, and by that means make her even weary of her life. But for a wife to refuse all pains of that kind, and to be discontented when her husband invites any friends, or when any come (as the three angels came to Abraham) unexpectedly, does not show that there is a loving affection in her, or a wifely subjection to her husband.

These faults are all the greater when the husband or wife is free and eager in entertaining their own kindred and friends, but reluctant and grudging about entertaining each other’s friends and kindred. From this commonly arises much resentment of one against the other, yes, much discord and contention between them; and from disliking one another’s practice in this, there often follows a dislike of one another’s person. So, as the fault is bad in itself, it proves to be much worse in the injuries that follow from it.

=== §. 44. #emph[Of husbands’ and wives’ mutual help in relieving the poor.]

The last common and joint duty in which husband and wife ought to be helpful to each other concerns those who are outside the house, namely the #emph[poor,] and such as stand in need of their help, who are to be #emph[relieved and supported.] Because man and wife usually eat their meals together, and are joint partakers of the good things God has created, they must put one another in mind of that precept of charity which was given to the Jews when they were at their meals: “send out portions to those who have nothing prepared.”#footnote[Neh 8:10.] The good wife, who is set forth by the Holy Spirit as a pattern and example for others to follow, and her husband are noted as being helpful to one another in this duty. For it is said of her that she stretches out her hand to the poor and to the needy;#footnote[Prov 31:20.] and it is said of him that he “praises her,”#footnote[#emph[v.] 28.] by this encouraging her to keep on doing the good things she did. What liberty the wife has, or how far she may be restrained if her husband utterly refuses to give consent, we will set out #footnote[#emph[Treatise] 3, §. 23. 33.] later in the particular duties of wives. The point noted here is this: that the husband himself must be bountiful to the poor according to his ability, and allow his wife, yes, urge her, to be so too, and also give her the means to be bountiful; and that the wife also must stir up her husband to generosity in this respect, and herself open her hand to the poor in the things which she may lawfully give.

There is nothing by which a man or wife can bring more profit to the house than by giving to the poor. What is given to the poor is “a loan to the #smallcaps[Lord],” and he will repay it with great increase.#footnote[Prov 19:17.] It is like #emph[seed,]#footnote[2 Cor 9:6.] which, being #emph[liberally sown,] will bring forth a #emph[plentiful] harvest. Yes, it is a means to make us #emph[friends,]#footnote[Luke 16:9.] who will speak a good word for us at the bar of Christ’s judgment seat. And it brings not only the #emph[blessing of men,]#footnote[Job 29:13; 31:20.] but that #emph[of God]#footnote[Matt 5:7.] also, even the greatest blessing of all, the #footnote[Matt 25:34.] blessing of eternal life. For it is a sacrifice with which “God is pleased.”#footnote[Heb 13:16.] The apostle notes this as one of Christ’s own sayings, which he left to his disciples by word of mouth: “It is more blessed to give than to receive.”#footnote[Acts 20:35.]

Besides, husbands and wives, in distributing alms, may receive #footnote[2\. Husband and wife in a position to give good direction to one another.] good direction from one another: the husband by telling the wife who are fit to be relieved (for husbands commonly know better those who are outside the family), the wife by telling the husband what things are best to give away. For wives commonly know which things there is the greatest store of, and what can best be spared in the house.

=== §. 45. #emph[Of husbands’ and wives’ unmercifulness to the poor.]

Contrary to this is the unmercifulness of many husbands, who #footnote[Vices contrary.] are not only hard-hearted themselves (never giving anything unless they are forced by the law of the land, and then they part #footnote[1\. Unmercifulness of the husband.] with what is given so grudgingly that it is not at all acceptable to God, for “God loves a cheerful giver”)#footnote[2 Cor 9:7.] but also tie their wives’ hands and allow them to give nothing. In this #footnote[2\. Binding his wife’s hands.] they bring on their own necks both the cry of the poor and the groans and grief of a merciful wife who is restrained like this, and aggravate their sin to a high degree.

On the other side, the unmercifulness of many wives is also contrary to the duty named above. For there are many who, though they have liberty to give from the common goods, and also an allowance of their own from which they may give, yet covetously hoard up all they can get and do not give a penny’s worth. They would rather let food and other things perish in the house, and throw them away when they have gone bad, than let anything be given out of the house while it is good. Shall not the created things that are spoiled in a house, and the poor who have gone without, raise a loud cry in the ears #footnote[Read Jas 5:1, 2. #emph[etc.]] of the Lord against them? Yes, further, many wives are grieved at their husbands’ generosity, and are always urging them to shut their hands and give no more. Are they not plain devils in this, opposing what is good?
